questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput Spring Boot application running on an embedded Tomcat container gradually experiences an increasing Old Generation baseline. Over several days, despite frequent Major GCs running, heap consumption creeps steadily toward 100% until the JVM crashes with `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space`. A heap dump inspection reveals that millions of `UserContext` instances and associated metadata byte arrays are retained via `ThreadLocalMap$Entry` references across standard worker threads. A code review isolates this interceptor:

java
@Component
public class UserContextInterceptor implements HandlerInterceptor {
    private static final ThreadLocal<UserContext> CONTEXT = new ThreadLocal<>();

    @Override
    public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response, Object handler) {
        String userId = request.getHeader("X-User-Id");
        if (userId != null) {
            CONTEXT.set(new UserContext(userId, new byte[64 * 1024])); // 64KB session cache
        }
        return true;
    }
}
